WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.260
Back to the 2026 planner from episode one.

2
00:00:03.297 --> 00:00:07.539
Its calendars are grids, with a
problem the recipe index never had:

3
00:00:07.539 --> 00:00:12.477
day 1 has to land under the right weekday,
and that's a different column every

4
00:00:12.477 --> 00:00:13.107
month.

5
00:00:13.319 --> 00:00:16.819
This is the Month View
template, previewing January.

6
00:00:16.946 --> 00:00:20.529
Thirty-one days in seven
columns, with Monday first.

7
00:00:20.561 --> 00:00:25.056
And January the first sits under
Thursday, after three blank cells.

8
00:00:25.264 --> 00:00:29.384
Click the calendar, and find
Offset, under Grid Configuration.

9
00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:32.790
Static would skip the same
number of cells in every month.

10
00:00:32.897 --> 00:00:34.757
This one is set to Dynamic.

11
00:00:34.965 --> 00:00:38.461
Dynamic reads a field from
the first day, weekday_num,

12
00:00:38.461 --> 00:00:41.365
and adds the number beside it: minus one.

13
00:00:41.461 --> 00:00:46.631
weekday_num starts counting at Sunday,
which is zero, so Thursday is 4.

14
00:00:46.765 --> 00:00:51.135
This calendar starts on Monday,
not Sunday, hence the minus one.

15
00:00:51.229 --> 00:00:53.868
Four, minus one: three blank cells.

16
00:00:54.075 --> 00:00:59.065
Switch it to Static, and day 1 drops
back into the first cell, under Monday.

17
00:00:59.203 --> 00:01:02.353
Back to Dynamic, and it
moves to Thursday again.

18
00:01:02.426 --> 00:01:04.456
That one setting does all the work.

19
00:01:05.519 --> 00:01:07.839
February is the interesting one.

20
00:01:07.968 --> 00:01:12.238
It starts on a Sunday, weekday
zero, so the sum is minus one.

21
00:01:12.385 --> 00:01:16.409
A negative offset wraps around
once, by the number of columns,

22
00:01:16.409 --> 00:01:20.358
and day 1 lands in the last
column, where Sunday belongs.

23
00:01:20.567 --> 00:01:23.583
Final Data Subset takes
a window of a list.

24
00:01:23.639 --> 00:01:26.735
The year page shows it best:
two grids, one source,

25
00:01:26.735 --> 00:01:29.049
the seven sections under the year.

26
00:01:29.086 --> 00:01:32.776
The first grid takes four,
starting at zero: the quarters.

27
00:01:32.984 --> 00:01:37.434
The second skips four and takes
three: Weeks, Notes and To-Do Lists.

28
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:40.480
Same children, two windows, no overlap.

29
00:01:40.687 --> 00:01:44.350
Now let's add a little calendar
of January to this page.

30
00:01:44.392 --> 00:01:47.629
G for grid, and drag out
some room under the lists.

31
00:01:48.185 --> 00:01:53.000
Its source is the year itself, and the
year's children are quarters, not days.

32
00:01:53.119 --> 00:01:55.329
Deep Traversal walks further down.

33
00:01:55.462 --> 00:01:59.592
Add Level, and take one child,
starting at zero: Quarter 1.

34
00:01:59.799 --> 00:02:02.809
Another level, one child again: January.

35
00:02:02.859 --> 00:02:06.299
And a third level with no
count, so it takes every day.

36
00:02:06.423 --> 00:02:09.217
Three levels down, from
the year to its days.

37
00:02:10.038 --> 00:02:13.905
Now the cells are January's
days, showing their full titles.

38
00:02:14.026 --> 00:02:18.666
To make a calendar, the Display Template
becomes day_num, just the number.

39
00:02:18.701 --> 00:02:25.457
Then seven columns, small gaps, and small
cells: 28 by 18 points, with 9 point text.

40
00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:34.390
And the same dynamic offset as
Month View: weekday_num, minus one.

41
00:02:34.449 --> 00:02:37.599
January the first moves
across to Thursday.

42
00:02:39.770 --> 00:02:43.310
Change the second level to
start at 1, and it's February,

43
00:02:43.310 --> 00:02:45.610
wrapped round to start on Sunday.

44
00:02:45.691 --> 00:02:48.541
And every cell is a link
to its own day page.

45
00:02:48.751 --> 00:02:50.101
One last tip.

46
00:02:50.237 --> 00:02:54.339
Ask a level for a child that isn't
there, a fourth month in a quarter,

47
00:02:54.339 --> 00:02:56.817
say, and the grid has nothing to show.

48
00:02:56.933 --> 00:03:00.752
The editor fills it with Item
placeholders, there's no error,

49
00:03:00.752 --> 00:03:02.943
and the PDF would show nothing.

50
00:03:02.989 --> 00:03:06.929
So when a grid goes blank, check
the hierarchy before the grid.

51
00:03:07.150 --> 00:03:11.865
Next time: references, and how one
page can appear in two places at once.
